---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<Fragment slot="head-scripts">
  {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="refrigerator-repair/amana-not-cooling"
  eyebrow="Amana Refrigerator Not Cooling · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="Amana Refrigerator Not Cooling? Simple Fixes First"
  subtitle="Amana is Whirlpool's value line — coils, start relay, evaporator fan, or defrost, and mostly simple parts. We'll give you the honest repair-vs-replace math. Here's what to check and how long your food stays safe."
  heroImageTitle="Amana Refrigerator Not Cooling Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A no-cool Amana is Whirlpool-platform parts — usually the simple, affordable ones.</h2>

    <p><strong>Amana is Whirlpool's value line, built on the Whirlpool platform, so a running-but-warm Amana comes down to the same short list: dirty condenser coils, a failed compressor start relay (often "clicking" at the back), a failed evaporator fan ("freezer cold, fridge warm"), a defrost system iced over, the control board, and the serious end — a locked compressor or sealed-system fault. Amana refrigerators are relatively simple, so most no-cool calls land on the cheap side: a coil clean or one inexpensive part. Clean the coils and check airflow first. Those are yours to do; the relay, board, and sealed system are a tech job. Because it's a value fridge, we'll always give you the honest repair-vs-replace math before you spend. A no-cool fridge is a same-day call — your food is on a clock. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Food is on a clock:</strong> per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door closed. Keep the doors shut, move critical items to a cooler with ice if you can, and get a tech out before that window closes. Full food-safety details are in the section below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 VALUE / REPAIR-VS-REPLACE DIFFERENTIATOR -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · The honest repair-vs-replace call</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">On a value fridge, the math matters — and we'll do it with you.</h2>

    <p>Amana is priced to be affordable, and that changes how you should think about a no-cool repair. The good news: most Amana faults are the cheap ones. A dirty-coil clean, a start relay, an evaporator fan, or a defrost part is modest to fix, and the fridge is otherwise fine — those repairs are almost always worth doing.</p>

    <p>The one fault that changes the math is a compressor or sealed-system failure on an older Amana. Because a value fridge doesn't cost much to replace, a sealed-system repair that approaches the price of a comparable new unit can tip the decision toward replacing. Our techs diagnose the exact fault first, then lay out the numbers plainly — repair cost versus a like-for-like replacement — so you decide with real information. We won't sell you a repair that doesn't make sense on a fridge at this price. It's the same platform, and the same straight talk, we bring to the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/whirlpool-not-cooling/">Whirlpool not cooling</a> page.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why an Amana stops cooling</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Dirty condenser coils (check first)</h3>
      <p>Coils caked in dust and pet hair can't shed the compressor's heat, so the fridge slowly loses cooling. The most common and most homeowner-fixable cause — and worse in an LA summer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Compressor start relay — the "clicking"</h3>
      <p>A click every few minutes from the bottom-rear with the fridge warming is often the start relay failing to start the compressor. A common Whirlpool-platform no-cool part, and an inexpensive one — but a tech diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Evaporator fan — "freezer cold, fridge warm"</h3>
      <p>The evaporator fan blows cold air from the freezer coil up into the fridge. When it fails or frost jams it, the freezer stays cold but the fridge warms. An affordable, worthwhile tech repair.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Defrost system iced over</h3>
      <p>A failed defrost heater, sensor, or control lets frost build on the evaporator until air can't pass and cooling drops. The tell is a wall of frost behind the freezer's back panel.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Compressor / sealed system</h3>
      <p>If the compressor runs but doesn't cool or won't start, the sealed system is the fault — the higher-ticket end, and the one repair where replacing a value Amana can be the smarter call. A gauges-and-tech diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before the food is at risk.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Vacuum the condenser coils</strong> behind the rear panel or under a front kick-plate. This alone fixes a lot of gradual no-cool cases.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the interior vents</strong> so food isn't blocking airflow between compartments.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door seal and temperature setting,</strong> and confirm the setting wasn't bumped warm.</li>
      <li><strong>Give it room and a power-cycle</strong> — pull the fridge off the wall, unplug a minute, restart.</li>
      <li><strong>Note the symptoms and the fridge's age</strong> — freezer cold while fridge is warm, clicking at the rear, or how old the unit is — so we can pinpoint the fault and run the repair-vs-replace math faster.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> coils, vents, seal, temperature, and a power-cycle are safe. Do not open the sealed refrigeration system, test the compressor or start relay, or replace the control board — those carry shock and refrigerant risks and are our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 FOOD SAFETY -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Heat-wave food safety — when to call today</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A warm fridge is a clock. Here are the numbers.</h2>

    <p>A refrigerator that's stopped cooling is a race against the clock, and in an LA heat wave that clock runs faster. Per USDA and FDA guidance:</p>

    <ul class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Refrigerator: about 4 hours</strong> of safe food with the door kept closed.</li>
      <li><strong>Freezer: roughly 48 hours full, 24 hours half-full,</strong> if the door stays shut.</li>
      <li><strong>Above 40°F for 2 hours or more → discard it.</strong> That covers meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, and leftovers.</li>
      <li><strong>When in doubt, throw it out</strong> — and never taste food to decide. Frozen food can be safely refrozen only if it still has ice crystals or is at 40°F or below.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>That's why a no-cool Amana is a same-day call. Keep the doors closed, move the critical items to a cooler with ice, and get a tech out before the four-hour fridge window closes. We answer phones 24/7 and run same-day across most of the area. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Coils clean, still warm? Now it's a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the coils are clean, the vents are clear, and the Amana still won't cool, the fault is inside — the start relay, the evaporator fan, the defrost system, or the compressor and sealed system. Because Amana shares the Whirlpool platform, our techs carry the common start relays, fan motors, and defrost parts for a one-visit fix where possible — and on a value fridge, we'll tell you honestly whether the repair or a replacement is the smarter money.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/not-cooling/">refrigerator not cooling</a> guide, the shared-platform <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/whirlpool-not-cooling/">Whirlpool not cooling</a> page, and the sibling brands <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/maytag-not-cooling/">Maytag</a> and <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/kitchenaid-not-cooling/">KitchenAid</a>. If it's the fridge warm while the freezer's fine, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/fridge-not-cooling-freezer-works/">fridge not cooling, freezer works</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/refrigerator-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Amana refrigerator not cooling: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Amana refrigerator not cooling? Call today — food is on a clock.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

